The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if (ret) {
    gen_pool_free(p2pdma->pool, (uintptr_t)kaddr, len);
    return ret;
}
```
Reason:
When `vm_insert_page()` fails inside the loop, the error path calls `gen_pool_free()` to free the allocated memory but does not call `percpu_ref_put(ref)` to release the per-CPU reference count that was acquired earlier (via `percpu_ref_tryget_live_rcu()`). This leaves the reference count elevated, causing `memunmap_pages()` to hang indefinitely when the PCI device is removed.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (ret) {
    gen_pool_free(p2pdma->pool, (uintptr_t)kaddr, len);
    percpu_ref_put(ref);
    return ret;
}
```
Reason:
Adding `percpu_ref_put(ref)` ensures that the per-CPU reference count acquired before the loop is properly released even when `vm_insert_page()` fails. This prevents the reference from remaining elevated, allowing `memunmap_pages()` to complete normally when the PCI device is removed.

